  weight to the point of emaciation the  premise is definitely scary but the  effect falls flat on the big screen  especially when we're confronted with  the actor in a terribly fake latex fat  suit that only gets slightly less  hilarious as he sheds two pounds Maximum  Overdrive  stephen king himself step behind the  lens for the 1986 adaptation of his own  Maximum Overdrive it was my first  pictures director I sort of enjoyed it  others however did not this movie puts  earth in the tail of a comet after which  once the dirt machines are suddenly come  to life and go on a murderous rampage  against humans  [Music]  that's a truckstop a bland protagonist  played by Emilio Estevez and an odd  assortment of other unremarkable  characters settle in to wait out the  living trucks to have them pinned down  inside while Maximum Overdrive does have  a killer soundtrack from ac/dc and a few  neat shots peppered throughout the movie  is still a maximum dud needful things  although the novel needful things as a  favorite among King fans the 1993 film  adaptation of the story might leave  audiences ready to sell their own souls  to the devil just to get it over with  set in Castle Rock main needful things  follows a character named Leland gaunt  opening an antique store called you  guessed it needful things many of the  town's residents soon find their heart's  desires fulfilled by the stores items  some of which have a distinctly  supernatural bent but gaunt prices are  nearly too good to be true  rather than heaps of cash gone just  wants to town people to play pranks on  each other but those small misdeeds end  up igniting some intense rivalries and  things turned violent very quickly if  that sounds like an intriguing story  that's because it is Kings written  version of needful things are certainly  worthy of your time but the movies  failure to translate the character  development of many players involved  renders this movie a total wash at least  we got a pretty great Rick and Morty  episode out of it a typewriter that  generates best-selling murder mysteries  and then makes the murders happen in  real life the Mangler tobe Hooper's 1994  adaptation of Kings short story the  Mangler somehow spanned the two sequels  but the original is not actually very  good the movie stars Freddy Krueger  actor Robert Englund as a sinister  laundry service owner whose machine has  been possessed by a demon that's been  activated by the presence of blood and  over the counter and assets yes really  the original short story was pretty  threadbare to begin with but the movie  completely ignored the opportunity to  make a campy picked up pokes fun itself  instead we're left with the ridiculous  and gory flick that goes on way too long  and takes itself much too seriously  riding the bullet when King published  his 2000 develop riding the bullet it  was generally well received by critics  and made history as the first mass  distributed  but the story follows Alan Parker an  erotic and depressed college student  whose obsession with worst-case  scenarios nearly drives him to suicide  after receiving news that his mother has  had a stroke Parker decides to hitchhike  so he can reach her before she passes  but instead of culminating in an  exciting thrill ride the movie renders  the journey completely boring add this  to your skip list dream catcher even  Stephen King that didn't completely love  his 2001 novel dream catcher but the  resulting movie was even worse the film  follows four lifelong friends who have  psychic powers of course on a winter  hunting trip to upstate Maine they soon  find themselves caught between invading  aliens and the US military forces trying  to contain them Thomas Jane Damian Lewis  Timothy Olyphant and Jason Lee do find  work as two friends but Morgan Freeman  falls flat as an overzealous commander  with ridiculously distracting eyebrows  what results as an overly long free  associating mess of a movie that could  have been much better if it had just  stuck to one tone the lawnmower man if  you're a fan of this short story from  King's 1978 collection nightshift don't  be fooled by Brad Leonard's 1992 film  that lawnmower man it's an adaptation  and name only other than the title and a  single seen the movie bears little  resemblance to the source material  instead of a nutty little tale about a  lawn mowing service gone horribly wrong  the film steals from the short story  flowers for algernon and Mary Shelley's  Frankenstein for its mish-mashed plot  the movie centers on a doctor who  chooses a mentally impaired greenskeeper  to be the subject of his experiments in  intelligence boosting via virtual  reality and a drug cocktail followed  King's lead on this one and give the  lousy story of the lawnmower man a hard  pass the knight flyer the knight flyer  stays fairly true to King's original  short story but that doesn't mean that  this 1997 adaptation is actually a good  movie miguel ferrer plays tabloid  reporter Richard Dee's who attempts to  hunt down a serial killer who uses a  small aircraft to travel from town to  town leaving a grisly trail of victims  in his wake these soon finds that this  is no ordinary criminal that must also  deal with the competing reporter who's  trying to hardest to steal a scoop while  the short stories version of  was an unlikable cynic who finds his  worldview shaken the movie script and  Ferraz flop performance makes d is a  complete jerk with no redeeming  qualities if you decide to watch the  night flyer you'll probably find  yourself rooting for the bad guy to win  before it's all said and done now that  we've gotten through the shlok here are  the best even king adaptation so far  carry for over 40 years Brian De Palma's  1976 adaptation of Carrie has remained  the classic released just two years  after the book's publication Carrie was  the first adaptation of a Stephen King  work and it's still arguably the best  featuring [ __ ] Spacek in the title role  this supernatural tale sets the hook of  suspense and reels and the viewer  without remorse Spacek as it turns  ethereal and terrifying as Carrie when  to Piper Laurie delivers a commanding  performance as a repressive mother  Margaret even though it's a little dated  today the original vision of Carrie  still holds up as a must watch Stephen  King movie stand by me  unlike many King adaptations stand by me  is more coming-of-age drama than horror  based on Kings a semi-autobiographical  short story the body it follows for  adolescent boys in a small Oregon town  who embark on a journey to find the  corpse of a missing boy thanks to the  off-screen friendship forged by actors  Wil Wheaton River Phoenix Jerry  O'Connell and Corey Feldman the  authenticity of the characters  connection was incredibly real stand by  me remains a classic today and even  those who don't like horror films can  enjoy the Stephen King story the dead  zone David Cronenberg's 1983 adaptation  of Stephen King's the dead zone remains  a more accessible movie than kings more  horrific works the story focuses on  Johnny Smith a man who wakes up from a  coma to discover he has unexplained a  psychic powers and finds himself on a  collision course with destiny during a  pivotal election it's a masterpiece of  thrilling and suspenseful storytelling  that strings viewers alone before the  final climactic scene the Shawshank  Redemption although it registered very  little impact in theaters upon its  release director Frank Darabont's 1994  prison drama the Shawshank Redemption  has gone on to become one of the  best-loved films of the last 30 years  the movie tells the story of two  prisoners serving life sentences  the Shawshank State Penitentiary you  become dear friends despite their  difficult circumstances starring Tim  Robbins and Morgan Freeman in the lead  roles  the Shawshank Redemption is a gritty  tale with a lot of heart and plot points  that transcend the generations The  Shining all work and no play might make  Jack a dull boy  but the shining most certainly isn't  dull Stanley Kubrick's 1980 version of  The Shining still stands as one of the  most beloved King adaptations even if  the author didn't care for himself the  movie follows writer Jack Torrance his  wife Wendy and their young son Danny as  they travel to Colorado to become the  winter caretakers of the opulent and  haunted Overlook Hotel Danny's psychic  abilities allow him to see the  terrifying pasts of the Overlook and his  troubled fathers and the demons are  exploited by the spirit still haunting  the hotel the tale deviates  significantly from the source material  in both characterization and storyline  nevertheless the shrining continues to  be a favorite among horror lovers misery  a psychological thriller directed by Rob  Reiner about fan obsession gone horribly  wrong 1990s misery was a critical and  commercial success that launched Kathy  Bates career and for good reason the  plot follows romance author Paul Sheldon  as he wakes up from a car accident to  find himself in the care of a super fan  named Annie Wilkes you and I were meant  to be together forever  when Wilkes learns that Sheldon has  killed off a favorite character in his  latest novel things start to get grim  and there are some scenes of torment  that won't soon be forgotten or forgiven  by fans of this film The Green Mile  director Frank Darabont returned to the  king library for another prison drama  with 1999's The Green Mile starring Tom  Hanks and Michael Clarke Duncan this  flashback film is told from the  perspective of Paul Edgecombe who  recounts the supernatural events  experienced as a corrections officer  after a very special prisoner arrived on  death row at the cold mountain  penitentiary  it's a soulful and thoughtful movie  which will leave most viewers profoundly  affected after the credits roll Dolores  Claiborne Kathy Bates returned to the  world of Stephen King when she appeared  in the 1995 psychological thriller the  Loras Claybourne based on the novel of  the  same name and directed by Taylor  Hackford the film follows the tale of a  domestic servant who's accused of  murdering her elderly employer  it's a harrowing and suspenseful tale  that will keep audiences guessing until  the end 14:08 in the 2007 psychological  horror film 1408 John Cusack does what  he does best he plays a cynic with love  problems starring as Mike Enslin a  skeptical author who decides to check in  to a notoriously haunted hotel room in  order to investigate it he's joined by  samuel l.jackson as hotel manager Gerald  Olin what follows is a tense and  terrifying descent into Enslin psyche  although it's definitely not on the  level of the shining 14:08 is still a  satisfying and scary haunted hotel  effort that manages to generate genuine  thrills without relying on bucketfuls of  gore it's the feature film debut of it  smashed all sorts of records on its  opening weekend and for good reason it's  a very decent adaptation of one of Kings  most prized books the 1990 TV  mini-series successfully translated the  scares of the novel as well but as  budgeted special effects and bloated  acting moments have kept that version  from aging well in the years since the  new version of it however is much  sharper and more intense and also  presents the hearts and weight of the  novel's characters scary clowns might be  a cultural phenomenon that makes  Pennywise a timely terror but counts on  this one to be more than just a flash in  the pan favorite - thanks for watching  click the loop icon to subscribe to our  YouTube channel plus check out all this  cool stuff we know your loved team  [Music]
 

